+# Design: knowledge bundle from authored Markdown
+
+## Goal
+
+People write documents in the app as Markdown (`SourceDocument`), together with a glossary
+and colloquial names. Publishing must turn that content into a `KnowledgeBundle`
+(`knowledge-bundle/v1`) so it goes through the existing bundle import
+(`build_snapshot` / `ImportKnowledgeBundle`) unchanged. The new module
+`pharma_agent.domain.corpus.authoring` does that conversion. It is pure and
+deterministic: the same content always gives the same bundle and the same
+`bundle_digest`.
+
+## Scope
+
+Included:
+
+- A new framework-free domain module `backend/src/pharma_agent/domain/corpus/authoring.py`
+  with `split_markdown`, `slugify`, `section_key`, `section_choices` and `build_bundle`.
+- Unit tests in `backend/tests/domain/corpus/test_authoring.py`.
+
+Unchanged:
+
+- The bundle schema and IO (`bundle.py`), `build_snapshot`, `bundle_digest`, chunking,
+  identities, the import use case, persistence and the API.
+- `SourceDocument`, `Collection`, `GlossaryEntry`, `ColloquialMappingRecord`.
+- No application or API wiring is added in this change; callers (the publish job) can
+  adopt `build_bundle` later.
+
+## Interfaces
+
+```python
+@dataclass(frozen=True)
+class MarkdownSection:
+    heading: str
+    path: tuple[str, ...]      # heading trail including this heading; () for the fallback section
+    ordinal: int               # 1-based, consecutive over kept sections
+    blocks: tuple[BlockRecord, ...]
+
+def split_markdown(markdown: str, *, fallback_heading: str) -> list[MarkdownSection]
+def slugify(text: str, *, max_length: int = 80) -> str
+def section_key(document_key: str, path: Sequence[str]) -> str
+def bundle_document_key(collection_key: str, document_key: str) -> str   # "<collection>:<document>"
+def section_choices(collection: Collection, documents: Sequence[SourceDocument]) -> list[tuple[str, str]]
+def build_bundle(
+    collection: Collection,
+    documents: Sequence[SourceDocument],
+    glossary: Sequence[GlossaryEntry],
+    colloquial: Sequence[ColloquialMappingRecord],
+) -> KnowledgeBundle
+```
+
+## Approach
+
+The module lives in the corpus domain next to `bundle.py` and `models.py`; it imports only
+the domain (`bundle`, `identity`, `models.Collection`, `source.SourceDocument`) and the
+standard library, so the layering tests keep passing.
+
+### Splitting Markdown
+
+- Input is normalised with `normalize_text` (NFC, `\n` line endings) first.
+- A line is a heading when it matches ATX syntax `^ {0,3}(#{1,6})[ \t]+(text)` (closing
+  `#`s stripped) and is not inside a fenced code block. Fences open with ``` or `~~~`
+  (three or more) and close with the same character and at least the same length.
+- `path` is maintained with a stack of `(level, heading)`: a new heading pops every entry of
+  the same or deeper level and is pushed. `path` is the stack's headings, so it includes
+  the section's own heading (matching `context_path` in existing bundles).
+- Text before the first heading, or a whole document without headings, forms a section
+  with `heading=fallback_heading` and `path=()`.
+- Within a section, consecutive lines whose stripped form starts with `|` (outside fences)
+  form one table block. Tables are numbered `t1`, `t2`, … across the whole document.
+  Text between tables becomes a prose block after `strip()`; empty prose is skipped.
+- Sections whose blocks end up empty are dropped; ordinals count kept sections only.
+
+### Keys
+
+- `slugify`: NFKD-decompose, map `đ`/`Đ` to `d`, drop combining marks and other non-ASCII,
+  lowercase, collapse every run of non `[a-z0-9]` into `-`, trim `-`, truncate to
+  `max_length` and trim `-` again.
+- `section_key(document_key, path)`: `f"{document_key}#{'/'.join(slugify(p) for p in path)}"`,
+  or `f"{document_key}#body"` for an empty path. A part whose slug is empty becomes
+  `section` so the key never has empty segments.
+- Bundle document keys are `f"{collection.key}:{document.key}"`, so section keys and chunk
+  ids never collide across collections.
+- Two sections in one document can share a heading path (e.g. two `## Ghi chú` under the
+  same parent). The second and later get `-2`, `-3`, … appended to the key, in reading
+  order. `section_choices` and `build_bundle` share one helper so both use the same keys.
+
+### Building the bundle
+
+- Documents are sorted by bundle key; each becomes a `DocumentRecord` with its kind,
+  title and `SourceInfo(title=source_title, url=source_url)`.
+- Each document is split with `fallback_heading=document.title`; each section becomes one
+  `SectionRecord` (`context_path=list(path)`, `ordinal`, `blocks`).
+- Glossary entries are copied as given. Colloquial mappings keep only section keys that
+  exist in the bundle; other fields are untouched.
+- Manifest: `collection` from the `Collection` key and title, generator
+  `pharma-agent-authoring` version `1`, document and section counts, `files={}`.
+  `build_id` and `source_digests["authored_content_sha256"]` are the sha256 of the
+  canonical content (as `bundle_digest`), so the manifest is deterministic too: no clocks,
+  UUIDs or randomness.
+
+### `section_choices`
+
+Pairs `(section key, label)` for every section of every document, documents in the given
+order and sections in reading order; label is `" › ".join((document.title, *path))`.
+
+## Testing
+
+Unit tests in `backend/tests/domain/corpus/test_authoring.py`:
+
+- `split_markdown`: heading paths and levels, fallback section, headings inside fences,
+  table runs and document-wide table numbering, stripped prose, dropped empty sections.
+- `slugify` (`Đường uống` → `duong-uong`, max length) and `section_key` (including `#body`).
+- `section_choices` keys and labels in reading order.
+- `build_bundle`: sorted collection-prefixed documents, section records, glossary as given,
+  unknown colloquial keys removed, manifest fields, equal `bundle_digest` for equal content
+  regardless of document order, and the bundle passes `write_bundle`/`read_bundle`
+  validation and `build_snapshot`.
+- Full backend suite (`uv run pytest`), including architecture tests.
